#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124241 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124437 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "049e8586-8348-50be-ac56-4ea79eba43cd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Slope of the Tangent Line to a Curve at a Point"}] :question/id #uuid "225b0bca-3520-58ab-bd36-0e3de337b604" :question/math-academy-id "e-3068" :question/problem "A surface $S$ is defined by $z=f(x,y).$ The curve $C$ is the intersection of $S$ with the plane $x=2.$ Given that the point $P(2,1,8)$ lies on $C,$ and that $f_y = \\dfrac{4x}y - 4x^2y,$ find the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=2.$ So, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P(2,1,8)$ will be given by the partial derivative $f_y(2,1).$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $f_y(x,y)$ at $(2,1)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(2,1) & =\\frac{4(2)}{(1)}-4(2)^{2}(1) \\\\  & =8-16 \\\\  & =-8\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124242 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124438 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c5685f7-71b2-58ae-8c61-f555b355d92f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of the Tangent Line to a Curve at a Point"}] :question/id #uuid "70bcf628-cf1f-5da0-85b7-19d0f514ccd8" :question/math-academy-id "e-3069" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=\\pi.$ Given that the point $P(1,\\pi,-1)$ lies on $C,$ and that $f_x = y\\cos(xy) + \\cos y,$ find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z = f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=\\pi.$ So, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(1,\\pi,-1)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\dfrac {\\partial z} {\\partial x} \\Bigg|_{(1,\\pi)}.$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\dfrac {\\partial z} {\\partial x}$ at $(1,\\pi)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(1,\\pi )} & =\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(1\\cdot \\pi )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi  \\\\  & =\\pi (-1)+(-1) \\\\  & =-\\pi -1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(1,\\pi )} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(1,\\pi )}(x-1) \\\\ z-(-1) & =(-\\pi -1)(x-1) \\\\ z+1 & =(-\\pi -1)x+\\pi +1 \\\\ z & =-(\\pi +1)x+\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation above represents a line in the $xz$-plane. The $xz$-plane through the point $P(1,\\pi,-1)$ has the equation $y=\\pi.$\n\nSo, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(1,\\pi,-1)$ is given by the intersection of the following planes:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=\\pi  \\\\ z=-(\\pi +1)x+\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124243 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124439 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0325887-ce25-5d6f-bf27-d85541e1dbe0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Tangent Vector to a Curve at a Point"}] :question/id #uuid "75c0deaf-38a7-5a81-afac-cae2978d52b4" :question/math-academy-id "e-3070" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=1.$ Given that the point $P(0,1,1)$ lies on $C,$ and that $f_x =  \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2} \\pi}{4} \\sin{\\left(2 x + \\dfrac{\\pi y}{4} \\right)},$ find a tangent vector to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $y=1.$ So, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative $f_x(0,1).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $f_x(x,y)$ at $(0,1)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(0,1) & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\pi }{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(0)+\\frac{\\pi (1)}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\pi }{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(0,1) & ={f}_{x}(0,1)(x-0) \\\\ z-1 & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}x \\\\ z & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}x+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation above represents a line in the $xz$-plane. The $xz$-plane through the point $P(0,1,1)$ has the equation $y=1.$\n\nSo, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(0,1,1)$ is given by the intersection of the following planes:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=1 \\\\ z=\\frac{\\pi }{4}x+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find a tangent vector, we can find a normal vector to each plane and compute their cross product.\n\n- For the plane $y = 1,$ we write $0x + 1y + 0z = 1$ and find the normal vector $\\mathbf{n}_1 = \\langle 0,1,0 \\rangle.$\n\n- For the plane $z = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4} x +1,$ we write $-\\dfrac{\\pi}{4} x + 0y + 1z = 1$ and find the normal vector $\\mathbf{n}_2 = \\left\\langle -\\dfrac{\\pi}4,0,1 \\right\\rangle.$\n\nComputing the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨0,1,0⟩\\times ⟨-\\frac{\\pi }{4},0,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -\\pi /4 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =i+\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt this point, we've found a tangent vector, and this could be our final answer. However, just to make our answer a little nicer, we can multiply the vector by $4$ to yield a simpler, parallel vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\cdot (i+\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,k)=4\\,i+\\pi \\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268885 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124438 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c5685f7-71b2-58ae-8c61-f555b355d92f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of the Tangent Line to a Curve at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3535f187-54bf-56fb-8443-7dab32a9c79d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}x=e \\\\ z=2y-3\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425099} {:answer/id #uuid "3c2c97aa-3243-5467-ac12-075c53d9654e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}x=e \\\\ z=y+3\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425100} {:answer/id #uuid "43046f4c-8850-5dea-9f71-c5e3ebec2500" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}x=e \\\\ z=2y-1\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425101} {:answer/id #uuid "5b8e160b-491c-5c8e-996d-6c8ececaab38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}x=e \\\\ z=y-2\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425102} {:answer/id #uuid "77cfa3de-e27b-5fe1-b628-9f80c5d48c58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}x=e \\\\ z=y+1\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425106}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "77cfa3de-e27b-5fe1-b628-9f80c5d48c58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}x=e \\\\ z=y+1\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425106} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ab8e018-d349-5c74-b2e8-1930a7b5eded" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268476}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5c5dd738-1c94-548e-aa53-3b5d32dc4abc" :question/math-academy-id "q-132589" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=e.$ Given that the point $P(e,2,3)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-\\frac{{e}^{y}}{{x}^{2}}$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=e.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(e,2,3)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(e,2)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}$ at $(e,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(e,2) & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡e-\\frac{{e}^{2}}{{e}^{2}} \\\\  & =2-1 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(e,2)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(e,2)}(y-2) \\\\ z-3 & =1(y-2) \\\\ z & =y+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(e,2,3)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=e \\\\ z=y+1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268891 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124439 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0325887-ce25-5d6f-bf27-d85541e1dbe0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Tangent Vector to a Curve at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1843e8dd-af0c-51f7-a63f-d47f4be0aaaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,0,5⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266343} {:answer/id #uuid "8ae2b17b-4d19-5b24-bc0c-59e77736ad05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2,0,3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267387} {:answer/id #uuid "9f6b280b-d5f7-568a-8d81-2dd2a22c34a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,0,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267583} {:answer/id #uuid "fb0faa48-7712-5417-80bf-a7c921e7985b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨3,0,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268366} {:answer/id #uuid "ffc676ee-4730-534b-a0c7-fe2034b61a7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨3,0,5⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268398}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1843e8dd-af0c-51f7-a63f-d47f4be0aaaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,0,5⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266343} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ba3e928-a7c0-5873-a82b-0e92f1edc942" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268631}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7da12630-1fa6-595d-a465-5095a1603e7f" :question/math-academy-id "q-132599" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=1.$ Given that the point $P(2,1,4)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}={e}^{y-1}+2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y-1)$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $y=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(2,1).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(2,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(2,1) & ={e}^{1-1}+2(2)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1-1) \\\\  & =1+4 \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(2,1) & ={f}_{x}(2,1)(x-2) \\\\ z-4 & =5(x-2) \\\\ z & =5x-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(2,1,4)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=1 \\\\ z=5x-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $y=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨0,1,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=5x-6$ (or $5x-z-6=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨5,0,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨0,1,0⟩\\times ⟨5,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-i-5\\,k \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,-5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $⟨-1,0,-5⟩$ is parallel to the tangent line, then $-⟨-1,0,-5⟩=⟨1,0,5⟩$ is also parallel to it."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124977 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124439 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0325887-ce25-5d6f-bf27-d85541e1dbe0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Tangent Vector to a Curve at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41ec35ef-bc6e-5ae3-be18-9b9ce4679dfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2,0,-3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321669} {:answer/id #uuid "8bdb8a5e-3b0d-5134-98cf-e35ce5f13f51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-1,1,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322265} {:answer/id #uuid "a0be3297-c3f7-5b45-9e19-ac84539d01ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,1,-2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322415} {:answer/id #uuid "b4dab9bc-d4e9-5357-b42b-704f2f3b619c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2,0,3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322559} {:answer/id #uuid "feac73a2-6cf2-5fa6-aa53-e2dafe29b2d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,0,6⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323157}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "feac73a2-6cf2-5fa6-aa53-e2dafe29b2d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,0,6⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323157} :answer-field/id #uuid "307ef176-8c07-5616-a330-828891c316a0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323269}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "38a2ac51-d5be-54ed-b565-78bd1a3a67e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-36371" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=-3.$ Given that the point $P(2,-3,3)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=6(x-1)$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $y=-3.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(2,-3).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(2,-3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(2,-3) & =6(2-1)=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(2,-3) & ={f}_{x}(2,-3)(x-2) \\\\ z-3 & =6(x-2) \\\\ z-3 & =6x-12 \\\\ z & =6x-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(1,1,0)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=-3 \\\\ z=6x-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $y=-3,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨0,1,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=6x-9$ (or $6x-z-9=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨6,0,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨0,1,0⟩\\times ⟨6,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-i-6\\,k \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,-6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $⟨-1,0,-6⟩$ is parallel to the tangent line, then $-⟨-1,0,-6⟩=⟨1,0,6⟩$ is also parallel to it."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391977 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124438 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c5685f7-71b2-58ae-8c61-f555b355d92f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of the Tangent Line to a Curve at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2df6297f-0710-55c1-aeca-d0bbd6193592" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}x=2 \\\\ z=5y+4\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436940} {:answer/id #uuid "46868586-3cca-540f-a185-1309d5aab5a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}y=0 \\\\ z=5x-14\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436942} {:answer/id #uuid "83e7b9f8-74d8-5b05-8940-fc35083f8350" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}y=0 \\\\ z=5x+6\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436945} {:answer/id #uuid "891f8685-7212-51ca-9476-5b254f301d07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}x=2 \\\\ z=-5x-6\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436946} {:answer/id #uuid "a94a09cf-cb2f-5cbc-9de1-d32e3178d6bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}y=0 \\\\ z=5x-6\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436947}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a94a09cf-cb2f-5cbc-9de1-d32e3178d6bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}y=0 \\\\ z=5x-6\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436947} :answer-field/id #uuid "2712fe89-2404-5ec9-a1bd-af26bb9d16b3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391978}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "376329be-a379-553e-b714-516f831f127f" :question/math-academy-id "q-36372" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=0.$ Given that the point $P(2,0,4)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}={e}^{y}+2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=0.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(2,0,4)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(2,0)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}$ at $(2,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(2,0) & ={e}^{(0)}+2(2)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0) \\\\  & =1+4 \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(2,0)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(2,0)}(x-2) \\\\ z-4 & =5(x-2) \\\\ z & =5x-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(2,0,4)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=0 \\\\ z=5x-6\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425118 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124437 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "049e8586-8348-50be-ac56-4ea79eba43cd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Slope of the Tangent Line to a Curve at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09b9a14b-262a-515e-bd82-1f704a821349" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425090} {:answer/id #uuid "2590094e-7f95-52df-bdd8-abb0af831110" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425096} {:answer/id #uuid "336e9f14-d6c6-5f83-897b-4c14eb89e5be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425097} {:answer/id #uuid "91000328-2b30-5049-9390-2fee6404bd0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425109} {:answer/id #uuid "e4a190e1-968a-5e68-8061-84d550c81b2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425113}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "09b9a14b-262a-515e-bd82-1f704a821349" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425090} :answer-field/id #uuid "4d50699f-3ed7-5626-8b63-c6ce285403be" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425116}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5cb4c36d-f79b-5525-8cf7-91f3e27f40c2" :question/math-academy-id "q-64966" :question/problem "A surface $S$ is defined by $z=f(x,y).$ The curve $C$ is the intersection of $S$ with the plane $y=1.$ Given that the point $P(0,1,0)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}={2}^{x+y}y(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+1)$, find the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P(0,1,0)$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(0,1).$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(0,1) & ={2}^{0+1}(1)(0\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+1)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436951 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124437 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "049e8586-8348-50be-ac56-4ea79eba43cd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Slope of the Tangent Line to a Curve at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e1e7001-fa28-52e7-9159-e1a9952ad01c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436941} {:answer/id #uuid "5212a9fa-3e07-5070-b235-7a3b34cb5bd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436943} {:answer/id #uuid "7ba5571e-63b9-59ee-98ea-36a27b8aac00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436944} {:answer/id #uuid "cc662000-df0c-5f2c-a156-05cd36f47ab9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436948} {:answer/id #uuid "e1a14c70-f4ee-5c8e-bd4a-ccddfe87a87e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436949}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5212a9fa-3e07-5070-b235-7a3b34cb5bd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436943} :answer-field/id #uuid "aee200cc-d9b2-5d5b-a23a-38088da4f688" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436950}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0063a739-db3e-5983-b65e-bb4c57fabf9c" :question/math-academy-id "q-64967" :question/problem "A surface $S$ is defined by $z=f(x,y).$ The curve $C$ is the intersection of $S$ with the plane $y=1.$ Given that the point $P$ with $x$-coordinate $\\pi $ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=y{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)$, find the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point where $x=\\pi $ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(\\pi ,1).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(\\pi ,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(\\pi ,1) & =(1){e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 1)}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 1) \\\\  & ={e}^{0}(-1) \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
